        "genericAuthType": "httpBearerAuth",
        "sendBody": true,
        "specifyBody": "json",
        "jsonBody": "={{ JSON.stringify({\n  model: \"openai/gpt-oss-20b\",\n  messages: [\n    {\n      role: \"system\",\n      content: \"You classify candidates into exactly one category based on their overall skill profile — think about what role this person would actually be hired for, not just keyword matching.\\n\\nCategories:\\n- automation-heavy: builds workflows, connects systems, or automates repetitive processes. Look for tools like n8n, Zapier, Make, RPA, or a pattern of scripting-for-automation even without a specific tool named.\\n- data: works primarily with data itself — analysis, pipelines, ML, or databases. Python + SQL + pandas-style skills usually land here, especially if automation tools are absent or secondary.\\n- web-dev: builds user-facing applications or APIs — frontend, backend web frameworks, or full-stack skills.\\n- other: skills don't clearly point to a specialization in any of the above (e.g. pure infrastructure/DevOps with nothing else, or too sparse to tell).\\n\\nA candidate with mixed skills should be classified by what they'd most likely be hired to do, not by which list has the most matches — e.g. someone with Python, SQL, and one scripting skill is a data person even if they also know a little JavaScript.\\n\\nExamples:\\n- Skills: [zapier, n8n, javascript] -> automation-heavy (workflow tools dominate their profile)\\n- Skills: [python, pandas, sql, docker] -> data (data-manipulation skills define them; docker is incidental infra)\\n- Skills: [react, fastapi, rest apis] -> web-dev (building applications, not automating or analyzing)\\n\\nRespond with ONLY a JSON array, no markdown, no code fences, no explanation. Format: [{\\\"id\\\":\\\"<uuid>\\\",\\\"category\\\":\\\"<category>\\\"}]\"\n    },\n    {\n      role: \"user\",\n      content: JSON.stringify($json.data.map(c => ({id: c.id, name: c.name, skills: c.skills})))\n    }\n  ],\n  temperature: 0.2\n}) }}",
        "options": {}
